hi I'm anyway and I'm a mathematics  teacher so one of the things I think is  centrally important to mathematics and  in some ways makes it a little bit  unique from learning some of the other  subjects is that making mistakes is so  important in fact it's something which  we spend our lives kind of fleeing away  from we don't like the feeling of all I  know this got wrong this is you get big  red X's on it and that's not a fun  feeling we've got very strong emotional  connections to that but actually it's  vitally important for us to engage the  part of our mind and form sort of neural  connections there that are associated  with okay I made a mistake something has  gone wrong number one how did I know  that was wrong number two how do I back  myself out of that and then think about  this from another perspective in you  know multiple representation theater we  talked about the importance the  centrality of seeing one idea a single  concept from multiple different angles  and in fact we don't know something well  until we can see it from multiple  perspectives if you think for example of  seeing a shadow or something and it  looks rectangular you think okay well I  have an idea in my mind of what that  looks like and then you look at that  same object its shadow from a different  perspective realize it's a circle and  suddenly your brain kind of does this  there's this cognitive dissonance that  you experience when you realize oh this  doesn't fit what I thought it was before  and the idea of a cylinder now finally  emerges into your mind you're like oh  without having thought about this from  multiple perspectives I wouldn't have  arrived at that understanding so I think  it's really important that students and  teachers embrace the ability to say okay  maybe I got it wrong the first time  that's okay what can I learn from  looking at this another way  [Music]
 

